        }
      ],
      "inputs": [
        {
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "type": "any"
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Recursive Workspace Instantiation\n\n> **Experimental plan:** this graph records an implementation attempt to inspect in the running workspace. It is not yet the final law of the model.\n\n## Governing rule\n\n**A border handle exposes a typed capability. Dragging it performs the operation defined by that capability while preserving the distinction between identity, representation, and relationship.**\n\nThe abstraction repeats at two scales:\n\n```text\nWorkspace container\n└── Graph presented as a macro-node\n    └── Graph container\n        └── Node\n```\n\nA graph contains nodes. A workspace contains graph instances backed by graph documents. Ports expose capabilities at either scale, and handles make those capabilities draggable.\n\n## One instantiate operation\n\nInstantiation is scale-independent. The receiving container determines the concrete primitive:\n\n| Drop target | Receiving scope | Result |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Blank space inside a graph | Graph container | Create a graph-owned node |\n| Blank workspace space | Workspace container | Create a graph instance backed by a graph document and declaration |\n| Compatible existing endpoint | Relationship layer at that scope | Perform the declared connection or relationship operation |\n| Interior of another open graph | That graph container | Create a node owned by that graph |\n\nBlank workspace space is not null. It is an active container surface, just as blank graph space is an active graph container surface.\n\n## Instance identity rule\n\n**Every `instantiate` drop creates an independent instance.** It allocates:\n\n- A new graph-local node record `id`.\n- A new semantic `data.identity.nodeId`.\n- Local position, fields, edges, and lifecycle owned by the receiving graph.\n- Source provenance and, where applicable, an `instanceOf` or class binding.\n\nDragging the same capability three times creates three different nodes with three different semantic identities. They may share the same source provenance, but they do not share identity or mutable state.\n\nAt workspace scale, every instantiate drop creates a distinct graph document, `graphId`, declaration record, semantic `nodeId`, and workspace instance. Only the explicit `declare` operation may create a new graph declaration that reuses an existing semantic `nodeId`.\n\n| Operation | Local record ID | Semantic nodeId |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `instantiate` | New on every drop | New on every drop |\n| `reference` | New portal/reference record | Points to a different existing identity |\n| `declare` | New declaration record | Reuses the explicitly selected semantic identity |\n| `consume-view` | Optional local representation carrier | Does not create a semantic instance |\n| `connect` | New edge record | Endpoint identities remain unchanged |\n\n## Deepest accepting container\n\nThe drop resolver chooses the deepest compatible target beneath the pointer:\n\n1. A compatible node or graph endpoint.\n2. An accepting graph interior.\n3. The workspace surface.\n\nAn endpoint drop performs relationship behavior. A graph-interior drop instantiates a node. A workspace-surface drop instantiates a graph and its declaration. The preview must name the exact result before mutation.\n\n## Capability dimensions\n\nThe capability carries independent policies for:\n\n- **Identity:** new local identity, explicitly shared semantic identity, or another declared identity policy.\n- **Representation:** selected view, surface, content, glyph, or class defaults.\n- **Relationship:** none, local edge, portal relationship, or another explicit endpoint operation.\n- **Authority:** what the source offers and what the receiving container may perform.\n- **Provenance:** source graph, workspace instance, node, port, surface, and capability version.\n\nContainer scale never silently chooses semantic identity. A node created inside a graph normally gets a new graph-local ID. A graph created on the workspace gets a graph document and declaration, but whether that declaration represents a new or existing semantic identity comes from the capability.\n\n## Grants and authorization\n\nSource ports grant semantic operations independently:\n\n+| Grant | Permitted operation |\n| --- | --- |\n| `navigate` | Open or focus the referenced graph or node |\n| `consume-view` | Render an exposed representation without creating semantic identity |\n| `reference` | Create a local portal or reference carrier pointing to the source |\n| `instantiate` | Create a new locally owned semantic identity from the exposed contract |\n| `declare` | Create another declaration of the same semantic identity |\n| `connect` | Establish a relationship with the exposed endpoint |\n| `invoke` | Execute an exposed runtime operation or tool |\n\nThese grants do not form a hierarchy. No grant implies another.\n\nThe receiving container independently authorizes mutations such as `create-node`, `create-graph`, `create-edge`, `update-node`, and `delete-node`. A transaction is permitted only when:\n\n```text\nsource port offers the required grant\n+ target accepts the capability\n+ target authorizes the required mutation\n= allowed transaction\n```\n\nFor example, `instantiate` plus graph `create-node` creates a new graph-owned identity. `instantiate` plus workspace `create-graph` creates a graph and declaration. `declare` plus workspace `create-graph` creates a graph whose declaration explicitly shares semantic identity. `reference` plus graph `create-node` creates a local portal or reference carrier.\n\n## Righting the current model\n\nThe runtime currently recognizes hierarchical authority values such as `navigate`, `consume-view`, `bridge`, and `runtime`, while drag creation is encoded primarily as `drag-create` plus `create-node`. Replace that inference gradually:\n\n1. Add a normalized capability resolver.\n2. Prefer explicit `grants`, identity, representation, and relationship policies.\n3. Translate legacy authority and behavior fields conservatively when explicit capabilities are absent.\n4. Route graph-level and workspace-level drag behavior through the normalized result.\n5. Update system defaults and authoring tools.\n6. Migrate canonical templates and public graphs in bounded batches.\n7. Remove hierarchical fallback only after compatibility tests and repository audits pass.\n\nThis lets us right the semantic model without breaking graphs that already depend on the current behavior.\n\n## Border projection\n\nThe port is the primitive projected onto a graph border. An output capability becomes a draggable source handle. An input capability becomes a target socket. A bidirectional port may provide both.\n\nViews, declarations, node classes, portals, content, glyphs, and other semantic nodes reach the border only through a declared public port. Edges never appear as independent draggable exports. Bridges may create incoming adjacency apertures, but they do not become creation capabilities unless a port explicitly grants that behavior.\n\nBorder handles remain stable while graph content semantic-zooms. Incoming adjacency occupies the top or left; outgoing or creatable capabilities occupy the right or bottom. The workspace minimap shows graph frames and adjacency, while graph-level navigation can still describe a large focused graph.\n\n## Transactions\n\nA graph-interior drop creates a node owned by the receiving graph, with a new graph-local ID unless identity policy says otherwise. A workspace drop creates a graph document, declaration, root port, workspace instance, placement, and focus. A compatible endpoint drop executes the declared relationship operation.\n\nEach result is one undoable transaction. Source graphs remain unchanged. Only the persistent receiving document becomes dirty. Workspace arrangement remains workspace state.\n\n## Remaining questions\n\n- What is the minimum authority vocabulary for offering and accepting capabilities at both scopes?\n- Which identity and provenance tuple survives repeated drops and multiple workspace instances?\n- Which concrete capability schemas should replace or extend the current `drag-create` plus `create-node` behavior without breaking existing graphs?\n\nThe first implementation should make the container resolver and capability interpreter explicit, preserve the existing local-edge model, and let us inspect the interaction before promoting this experiment into a durable contract."
        },
        "interfaceContract": {
          "version": 1,
          "receivesViewContent": true
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "workspace-graph-node-projection-declaration"
        }
      }
    },
